<article class="enc-article">
<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Al-Hadi ila'l-Haqq Yahya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/859">859</a>–<a href="/years/911">911</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Yahya ibn al-Husayn</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Hadi ila'l-Haqq Yahya ibn al-Husayn was the founder of the Zaydi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">imamate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>. A descendant of the Prophet's grandson al-Hasan from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a>, he came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saada">Sa'dah</a> in 897 at the invitation of feuding tribes, settled their disputes and set up a state there. His writings founded the Hadawi school of Zaydi law, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imams</a> claiming his legacy ruled in Yemen until 1962.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-hadi-ila-l-haqq-yahya.jpg" alt="Al-Hadi ila'l-Haqq Yahya" width="320" height="155" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Unknown, medieval mint-master. Photo: CNG coins · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dinar_of_al-Hadi_ila%27l-Haqq,_AH_298.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Later Zaydi sources place his birth in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, but he was probably born in a village near the wadi al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras">Rass</a>, where his grandfather al-Qasim al-Rassi, one of the chief authorities of the Zaydi school, had settled after bringing the family from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> around 827. By seventeen Yahya could compose treatises and issue judgments, and his family came to see him as a candidate for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">imamate</a>, which in Zaydi doctrine any qualified descendant of Fatimah could claim by rising and calling for allegiance.</p><p>Past Shi'a risings against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> had failed, while Zaydi states had taken root on the periphery, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a>. Between 884 and 889 Yahya and his family stayed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amol">Amol</a> in Tabaristan, but rumours that he would claim the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">imamate</a> roused the suspicion of its ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-zayd">Muhammad ibn Zayd</a>, and a plot to arrest them forced them to flee back to the Hejaz.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> was then fragmented: the Yu'firid dynasty held much of the interior and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanaa">Sana'a</a>, the Ziyadids held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zabid">Zabid</a>, and warring tribes owed allegiance to no one in the north. A first journey in 893/4, at the invitation of the Banu Futaym clan, ended when his own men grew undisciplined. He returned and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saada">Sa'dah</a> on 15 March 897, took the title of commander of the faithful and the name al-Hadi, and in July 897 subdued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najran">Najran</a> region.</p><p>The struggle for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanaa">Sana'a</a> dominated the rest of his life. He entered the city on 19 January 901 and struck coins in his name, but it changed hands repeatedly with the Yu'firids, and in May 902, in poor health, he was carried back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saada">Sa'dah</a> in a litter. After the Isma'ili missionary Ali ibn al-Fadl took Sana'a in November 905, al-Hadi held it again from April to November 906 and from 7 April to 23 June 910, while tribal revolts, above all by the Banu'l-Harith of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najran">Najran</a>, troubled his rule.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Al-Hadi died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saada">Sa'dah</a> on 18 August 911 and was succeeded by his son Muhammad. His tomb beside the al-Hadi mosque, one of the oldest buildings of Islamic Yemen, has been a place of Zaydi pilgrimage since. Of the 73 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imams</a> of Yemen who followed him, 60 were his direct descendants. His jurisprudence survives in his unfinished Kitāb al-Aḥkām and the Kitāb al-Muntakhab compiled by his follower Muhammad ibn Sulayman al-Kufi, and in theology he drew mostly on Mu'tazili doctrine. His strict code, such as the veiling of women, did not always please tribes who had wanted a mediator rather than a reformer.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Al-Hadi ila'l-Haqq Yahya” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 897 <b>Yahya ibn al-Husayn</b>, a descendant of al-Hasan from the Hejaz, came to Sa'dah at the invitation of feuding tribes.</blockquote><a href="/state/zaydi-imamate-of-yemen">Zaydi Imamate of Yemen</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ar Rass, in what is now <a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saada">Saada</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/yemen">Yemen</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=25.87,43.5&amp;year=892" aria-label="Ar Rass on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5700 1213 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Al-Hadi ila'l-Haqq Yahya"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6150 1483h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6150 1483h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=25.87,43.5&amp;year=892">What was here in 892 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
